/*
 * Copyright 2026 NXP
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 *
 * System-PM constraint layer: mark which SoC power states stop the LPADC from
 * completing a conversion. When the driver holds a conversion lock, the PM
 * policy must not pick one of these states. Stacked on top of the per-board
 * overlay in boards/ for the system phase (via EXTRA_DTC_OVERLAY_FILE, so the
 * board overlay is still picked up automatically).
 *
 * Every MCXN and MCXA SoC declares the same four power states -- sleep,
 * deepsleep, powerdown, deeppowerdown -- so this file is family-wide.
 *
 * Per MCX N23x RM Rev 4 Table 220 (and the equivalent table in every other MCXN
 * and MCXA RM): the ADC analog block is not active in Power Down and the whole
 * CORE domain (registers included) is gated in Deep Power Down, so neither can
 * be entered mid-conversion. Deep Sleep is listed as well: the converter is
 * allowed to keep running there (CTRL[DOZEN] = 0 by reset, bus clock gated but
 * hardware trigger and compare wakeup still live), yet only for as long as ADCK
 * survives, and the default clock configuration makes no such promise. A project
 * that arranges a low-power ADCK source can drop &deepsleep.
 *
 * Every MCXN/MCXA SoC DTS now carries this exact list, so on those SoCs this
 * overlay only restates what the SoC already declares. It is kept because the
 * system phase is about the constraint mechanism, not about who declared it: the
 * phase still has to pass on a board whose SoC DTS predates that list.
 */

&lpadc0 {
	zephyr,disabling-power-states = <&deepsleep &powerdown &deeppowerdown>;
};
